# A VBA module is a Windows text format, and it must reach a user as one.
#
# Without this, the .bas is stored LF and checked out however the local
# core.autocrlf happens to be set. That is not a cosmetic difference: a
# developer on Windows with autocrlf=true edits and tests a CRLF file, while a
# CI runner checks out LF and packages *that* - so the file a user imports is
# not the file anyone validated. The VBA editor writes CRLF when it exports a
# module, so CRLF is what a .bas is.
#
# `eol=crlf` normalizes to LF in the repository and converts on checkout on
# every platform, which makes the working tree and the release package agree no
# matter who builds it.
*.bas text eol=crlf

# The release archive carries the docs and the header verbatim. They are read on
# Windows, by Notepad among other things, so they get the same treatment.
*.md text eol=crlf
*.h text eol=crlf
